.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt MOUNT_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount_minixfs
.Nd mount a MINIX file system image with FUSE
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl T Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Ar FUSE options
.Ar image
.Ar mountpoint
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
mounts the MINIX file system in
.Ar image
on
.Ar mountpoint
through the high-level FUSE interface, which libfuse on Linux and
.Fx
and librefuse on
.Nx
and MINIX 3 provide.
Every version, V1, V2 and V3, in either byte order, and the file systems
of Minix-vmd are read; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.Pp
The mount is read-only and single-threaded: the options
.Fl s
and
.Fl o Ar ro
are always given to FUSE.
Inode numbers, modes, link counts, owners, times and device numbers are
those of the image.
The image is opened before FUSE takes over, so a relative path works.
Other options, such as
.Fl f
to stay in the foreground or
.Fl o Ar allow_other ,
are passed on to FUSE.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl T Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Read an image that holds the file system in the tracks of one side of a
disk only, as for
.Xr minixfs 1 .
.El
.Pp
The file system is unmounted with
.Ql fusermount3 -u Ar mountpoint
on Linux, or with
.Xr umount 8
on the BSDs.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 when the file system has been unmounted, 1 if the image
cannot be opened or FUSE fails, and 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mount_minixfs usr.img /mnt
$ ls -l /mnt
$ fusermount3 -u /mnt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh BUGS
The file system cannot be written.
.Pp
On
.Nx
10.1, looking up
.Ql \&..
in a directory right below the root makes librefuse free the node of the
root, after which lookups fail, so the mount is of little use there.
